  "worked_solution": "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{\u2032}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{\u2034}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+\u22ef,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=x{e}^{x+2}$ and $a=-1.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =x{e}^{x+2} \\\\ f(-1) & =(-1){e}^{1}=-e \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & ={e}^{x+2}+x{e}^{x+2}=(1+x){e}^{x+2} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(-1) & =(1-1){e}^{1}=0 \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & ={e}^{x+2}+(1+x){e}^{x+2}=(2+x){e}^{x+2} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(-1) & =(2-1){e}^{1}=e \\\\ {f}^{\u2034}(x) & ={e}^{x+2}+(2+x){e}^{x+2}=(3+x){e}^{x+2} \\\\ {f}^{\u2034}(-1) & =(3-1){e}^{1}=2e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{x+2} & =-e+(0)(x+1)+\\frac{e}{2!}(x+1)^{2}+\\frac{2e}{3!}(x+1)^{3}\u22ef \\\\  & =-e+\\frac{e}{2}(x+1)^{2}+\\frac{e}{3}(x+1)^{3}\u22ef.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
